 > Just wondering if anyone knows how to edit the
 > files required to get these patches working
 > together. Thanks.

Well, it helps a lot if you can read and write C
code yourself, because the answer is that you do all
the patches that failed to apply, by hand, yourself.

It is just _barely_ possible to do the job without
those skills.

You have to go into the code, look at a patch hunk that
didn't apply (they are listed when you run "patch"),
notice the code that got changed (usually, the part
in the patch hunk that comes first, prefaced by "-"
or "!"), notice how it got changed (the part
prefaced by "+" or another "!"), find the code that
was changed (which will have moved up or down in the
source code file, but might be at least close to the
originally listed change lines), take out exactly
what was intended to be taken out, put in exactly
what was intended to be put in. If you do this for
each patch hunk that failed, and you are profoundly
careful and lucky, the result should now compile for
you.

Better is to get a manual for diff and patch and
read more advice on the subject than I care to write
here. This link in the middle of the GNU diff/patch
documentation may be helpful:

http://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_mono/diff.html#Imperfect

This writeup is better than the one I just gave:

http://community.pennmush.org/node/72

